# Short identifiers are often clearer for indexes, iterators, and dimensions.
# Trailing return types conflict with the project's existing declaration style.
# Named constants for every numeric literal add noise in numerical code and tests.
# Cognitive-complexity thresholds are subjective and duplicate code review.
# Implicit boolean conversions are idiomatic for streams and smart handles.
# Swappable-parameter warnings are noisy for small internal functions and C APIs.
# Raw-string rewrites are a readability preference, not a modernization issue.
# Explicit types are intentional when they document numerical precision.
Checks: '-*,bugprone-*,performance-*,modernize-*,readability-*,-readability-identifier-length,-modernize-use-trailing-return-type,-readability-magic-numbers,-readability-function-cognitive-complexity,-readability-implicit-bool-conversion,-bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,-modernize-raw-string-literal,-modernize-use-auto'
WarningsAsErrors: '*'
